WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:03.002
-Our next comedian
is a wonderful director

00:00:03.002 --> 00:00:04.503
who's kicking off
his latest tour,

00:00:04.503 --> 00:00:06.372
Please Stop the Ride
in Boston,

00:00:06.372 --> 00:00:08.607
Seattle and Walla Walla,
Washington.

00:00:08.607 --> 00:00:11.076
And his special,
"The Old Man and the Pool,"

00:00:11.076 --> 00:00:13.512
is streaming now on Netflix.
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.512 --> 00:00:15.949
-There was this sign growing
up that said "please shower

00:00:15.949 --> 00:00:18.718
before entering the the pool."
And I feel like that was written

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:20.486
for one guy,
you know what I mean?

00:00:20.486 --> 00:00:24.324
Like, I think the first draft
of that one said "Greg."

00:00:24.324 --> 00:00:28.161
[ Laughter ]

00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:29.796
-Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:29.796 --> 00:00:32.031
our very good friend
Mike Birbiglia.

00:00:32.031 --> 00:00:33.566
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:45.444 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:45.444 --> 00:00:47.580
How are you?
-I'm good.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.048
-Wonderful to be here.
-Great to see you.

00:00:49.048 --> 00:00:52.218
-I'm very excited about
the tour.

00:00:52.218 --> 00:00:54.187
One of the first stops
is Walla Walla, Washington,

00:00:54.187 --> 00:00:56.589
which has great
historical significance to you.

00:00:56.589 --> 00:00:59.058
-I have a personal story
that occurred

00:00:59.058 --> 00:01:01.661
in Walla Walla, Washington,
almost 20 years ago,

00:01:01.661 --> 00:01:05.831
which is -- people are going to
think I'm making this up.

00:01:05.831 --> 00:01:08.334
But I jumped through a second
story window sleepwalking.

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:10.236
-Yeah.
-Of a -- true story --

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:12.005
of a La Quinta Inn.

00:01:12.005 --> 00:01:13.940
[ Laughter ]

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:15.141
So this is sort of a --

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:17.610
-They have that problem
a lot at La Quinta.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:21.314
-So this is sort of an ad.
[ Laughs ]

00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:23.215
It's a true story.

00:01:23.215 --> 00:01:24.650
Some people correct me.
They go, "No, no, no."

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:28.087
When I say La "Qwinta,"
they go, "No, it's La 'Kinta.'"

00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:31.591
I'm like, you can't
force me to speak Spanish.

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:33.993
I didn't press
two or whatever,

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:36.463
but, uh, so no,
20 years ago,

00:01:36.463 --> 00:01:39.231
I jumped through
a La Quinta Inn window.

00:01:39.231 --> 00:01:42.568
-Which was then made famous.
You did a special, a movie,

00:01:42.568 --> 00:01:44.204
"Sleepwalk with Me,"
which is wonderful.

00:01:44.204 --> 00:01:45.838
-And then yeah, and so I --

00:01:45.838 --> 00:01:49.408
I'm going back to Walla Walla.
I haven't been there.

00:01:49.408 --> 00:01:51.844
So I was like,
I'm going to...

00:01:51.844 --> 00:01:54.013
I've researched theaters,

00:01:54.013 --> 00:01:56.716
and there's this beautiful place
called the Power House Theatre,

00:01:56.716 --> 00:01:58.251
and I'm doing a couple shows

00:01:58.251 --> 00:02:00.419
and I'm going to bring
a camera and film myself,

00:02:00.419 --> 00:02:02.255
like, going back
to the motel room.

00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:05.124
I found out -- true story --
No.

00:02:05.124 --> 00:02:09.094
And so I called to rent
the room.

00:02:09.094 --> 00:02:12.665
Because we actually put up
a plaque next to the room.

00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:14.700
-This is also a true story.
-This is true.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.804
-So you just sent this to
the La Quinta Inn.

00:02:18.804 --> 00:02:21.274
-Yeah.
So it says "In this room,

00:02:21.274 --> 00:02:24.310
on January 26, 2005,
comedian Mike Birbiglia

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.111
sleepwalked out the window.

00:02:26.111 --> 00:02:28.247
That story became the basis
for the book

00:02:28.247 --> 00:02:29.381
and feature film
'Sleepwalk with Me.'

00:02:29.381 --> 00:02:31.217
Seriously. Google it."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:31.217 --> 00:02:34.687
And that's real.
And sometimes people go,

00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:36.989
fans of mine will make
the trip to Walla Walla

00:02:36.989 --> 00:02:39.358
and they'll stay in the room,

00:02:39.358 --> 00:02:44.030
which is like
the weirdest attraction ever.

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:45.331
-I can't believe they put it up.

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:47.233
It's really -- I mean,
I think that's a real honor

00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:48.801
that they were like,
I mean, I guess they probably

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:50.536
almost never get a plaque sent.

00:02:50.536 --> 00:02:51.804
-Well, that's the thing.

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:54.473
You can make plaques,
you can make trophies.

00:02:54.473 --> 00:02:57.610
I mean, my new
special "Old Man and the Pool"

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.511
is up for a Critics' Choice
award in January.

00:02:59.511 --> 00:03:00.746
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:00.746 --> 00:03:03.082
-I'm not going to win.
I'm not going to win.

00:03:03.082 --> 00:03:04.550
But I'm going to make
myself a plaque afterwards.

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.886
-Why not? Right?
[ Laughter ]

00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:09.555
Why not?
-No one's going to contest that.

00:03:09.555 --> 00:03:11.824
-It's not like the plaque
makers are like, "But did you?"

00:03:11.824 --> 00:03:13.259
-"But did you?

00:03:13.259 --> 00:03:14.493
We're going to have
to see some paperwork."

00:03:14.493 --> 00:03:17.096
-"There's some integrity
in the plaque industry."

00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:20.333
-No. And so I'm going back

00:03:20.333 --> 00:03:23.703
and I -- So, I called
the La Quinta Inn

00:03:23.703 --> 00:03:25.971
and I said, "Hey,
I'd like to rent this room"

00:03:25.971 --> 00:03:29.842
and then it's not
a La Quinta Inn anymore.

00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:33.345
They're like,
"That's not this motel,

00:03:33.345 --> 00:03:34.746
that's another motel."

00:03:34.746 --> 00:03:37.049
They said that's a -- I think
that's a Comfort Inn now.

00:03:37.049 --> 00:03:38.784
-Okay.
-So I call the Comfort Inn.

00:03:38.784 --> 00:03:40.620
I go, "Hey, I jumped through
the second story window."

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:41.854
[ Laughter ]

00:03:41.854 --> 00:03:44.923
I go, "I believe
there's a plaque on the wall."

00:03:44.923 --> 00:03:47.827
They go, "No, no, that's
actually the Quality Inn."

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:50.329
-Wow.
-I call the Quality Inn.

00:03:50.329 --> 00:03:54.100
[ Laughter ]

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:55.268
True story.

00:03:55.268 --> 00:03:57.470
I call the Quality Inn.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:59.171
[ Laughter ]

00:03:59.171 --> 00:04:00.739
I go, "I'm Mike Birbiglia.

00:04:00.739 --> 00:04:04.410
I believe there's a plaque
on the wall of me."

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.546
They say, "Oh, yes,
there it is."

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:08.714
I go, "I'd like
to rent that room."

00:04:08.714 --> 00:04:10.183
And the guy goes,
"Could you hold?

00:04:10.183 --> 00:04:13.119
I'm going to talk
to my manager for a second."

00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:14.419
True story.
And I'm like, "Oh, okay.

00:04:14.419 --> 00:04:15.654
'I'm going to talk
to the manager.'

00:04:15.654 --> 00:04:17.790
They're going to roll out
the red carpet."

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.493
The way we all know
the Quality Inn does.

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:21.727
[ Laughter ]

00:04:21.727 --> 00:04:23.129
-Step one, I think they're
going to have to go

00:04:23.129 --> 00:04:24.130
buy a red carpet.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.999
[ Laughter ]

00:04:26.999 --> 00:04:29.902
-Step two, the carpet
is already red from the blood.

00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:31.603
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:31.603 --> 00:04:34.573
You're about to
stay there, Mike!

00:04:34.573 --> 00:04:35.842
-So the --

00:04:35.842 --> 00:04:38.811
So the manager comes,
the man, he goes,

00:04:38.811 --> 00:04:40.313
"I got to talk to my manager."

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:42.548
I'm thinking it's going
to be a big deal.

00:04:42.548 --> 00:04:44.750
Like, they're going to make
something -- Comes back.

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:46.386
He goes, "Great news.

00:04:46.386 --> 00:04:48.287
We're going to give you 10% off
the room."

00:04:48.287 --> 00:04:50.556
-Wow.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:50.556 --> 00:04:53.592
That's quality.
-So the lesson here,

00:04:53.592 --> 00:04:57.096
if you want 10% off your room
at Quality Inn,

00:04:57.096 --> 00:05:00.800
you need to either
join Quality Inn Rewards

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.802
or jump through
their second story window.

00:05:03.802 --> 00:05:05.971
[ Applause ]
And then you get the red carpet.

00:05:05.971 --> 00:05:08.775
-Then you get the red carpet.
-God knows how it became red.

00:05:08.775 --> 00:05:11.844
-I just like at some point
I feel like all the Walla Walla

00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:13.212
hotels were calling around
being like,

00:05:13.212 --> 00:05:15.047
"Just FYI, there's a weird guy."
-I know.

00:05:15.047 --> 00:05:17.350
-"He's just calling up
saying he sent a plaque."

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:19.819
-Such a bizarre -- I mean,
it truly is like

00:05:19.819 --> 00:05:22.020
one of the more bizarre things
you could imagine occurring.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.022
-I know,
but it's all by your hand.

00:05:24.022 --> 00:05:25.858
Like, you're talking about it
like they were weird.

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:27.326
[ Laughter ]
-Right.

00:05:27.326 --> 00:05:28.527
-Like you're the guy who, one,

00:05:28.527 --> 00:05:31.129
walked out a window,
two, sent a plaque,

00:05:31.129 --> 00:05:32.498
and then, three, were
calling around like you

00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:34.300
were the king of hotels.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:36.001
-No, you know
what's great about it, too,

00:05:36.001 --> 00:05:38.638
is I told the story recently.

00:05:38.638 --> 00:05:40.473
And I'm going to
tell it in Walla Walla.

00:05:40.473 --> 00:05:41.674
But I always tell the story.

00:05:41.674 --> 00:05:43.542
I go, "I jumped
through the window,

00:05:43.542 --> 00:05:46.512
I'm bleeding, you know,
I'm in my underwear."

00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:48.147
I'm like, "I need to walk
to the front desk

00:05:48.147 --> 00:05:50.049
and explain what happened."

00:05:50.049 --> 00:05:53.719
True. I walked up
to the front desk and I go --

00:05:53.719 --> 00:05:57.022
Fortunately, it wasn't
the smartest person

00:05:57.022 --> 00:05:58.524
I've ever encountered,

00:05:58.524 --> 00:06:00.826
and I feel like he might have
felt that way about me as well.

00:06:00.826 --> 00:06:03.329
-Sure, sure. Yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:03.329 --> 00:06:04.930
-But I'm going back.

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:06.532
I'm going to --
I'm actually going to try to

00:06:06.532 --> 00:06:09.335
find the desk clerk.

00:06:09.335 --> 00:06:11.537
He messaged me on Instagram
at one point.

00:06:13.438 --> 00:06:15.842
I'm going to try
to find the doctor

00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:19.712
who puts stitches in my legs,
who also, like, reached out.

00:06:19.712 --> 00:06:21.480
Everybody -- It's a community.
-Yeah.

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:22.782
[ Laughter ]

00:06:22.782 --> 00:06:25.918
I bet they all remember it.

00:06:25.918 --> 00:06:28.987
-Every 20 years.

00:06:28.987 --> 00:06:30.656
No, it was the first time

00:06:30.656 --> 00:06:32.658
we're all getting the team
back together.

00:06:32.658 --> 00:06:34.760
[ Laughter ]
We're going to re-enact it.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:36.128
-Yeah.
-You know.

00:06:36.128 --> 00:06:37.630
-Speaking of doctors,
"Old Man and the Pool,"

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:39.132
which is wonderful.
-Yeah. Thanks so much.

00:06:39.132 --> 00:06:41.066
-I saw it live,
and now it's on Netflix.

00:06:41.066 --> 00:06:42.135
-You brought your mom.

00:06:42.135 --> 00:06:43.769
-I did, I brought my mom
and my brother.

00:06:43.769 --> 00:06:46.071
We loved it.
You're the -- You talk about

00:06:46.071 --> 00:06:48.373
a doctor basically says
you should start swimming

00:06:48.373 --> 00:06:49.575
and that's the story.

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:51.610
But your actual doctor
came and saw the show.

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:53.345
-First of all, my doctor came.

00:06:53.345 --> 00:06:54.746
First of all, this morning --

00:06:54.746 --> 00:06:57.783
This is how unintimidating
I am as a celebrity.

00:06:57.783 --> 00:07:00.386
No one ever is intimidated
to be like,

00:07:00.386 --> 00:07:03.122
"We shouldn't go up
and say hi to him.

00:07:03.122 --> 00:07:06.392
It might go badly." Literally
someone today goes,

00:07:06.392 --> 00:07:09.829
"Hey, I haven't seen you
at the YMCA pool lately."

00:07:09.829 --> 00:07:13.499
I'm like, "I don't know you.
How are you this --

00:07:13.499 --> 00:07:17.503
How are you -- I'm just..."
-Yeah.

00:07:17.503 --> 00:07:20.105
-I think people view me
like a wild animal.

00:07:20.105 --> 00:07:23.142
They're like,
"It's more afraid of you

00:07:23.142 --> 00:07:26.212
than you are of it."
[ Laughter ]

00:07:26.212 --> 00:07:28.881
Like, you know,
the walrus is around.

00:07:28.881 --> 00:07:31.150
You can talk to him.
You know what I mean?

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:33.953
No, but, yeah,
my doctor came, which was great.

00:07:33.953 --> 00:07:35.955
I have a great doctor,
and I'm really lucky.

00:07:35.955 --> 00:07:38.057
But she did, like,
after she saw the show,

00:07:38.057 --> 00:07:41.393
I talked about how I had bladder
cancer, type two diabetes.

00:07:41.393 --> 00:07:43.996
I sleepwalked through
a second story window.

00:07:43.996 --> 00:07:46.198
After she saw the show,

00:07:46.198 --> 00:07:50.503
she was, I think, much more
concerned during our visits

00:07:50.503 --> 00:07:54.307
because I think when you see it
in a one-hour format,

00:07:54.307 --> 00:07:57.643
it's like
I'm performing my chart.

00:07:57.643 --> 00:07:59.111
-Yeah.
-You know what I mean?

00:07:59.111 --> 00:08:02.281
Nobody usually gets a Netflix
special about their maladies.

00:08:02.281 --> 00:08:03.648
-Yeah.
-You know what I mean?

00:08:03.648 --> 00:08:05.083
-Right. When you see it
on a clipboard as a doctor,

00:08:05.083 --> 00:08:06.452
you're like,
"I can deal with all this."

00:08:06.452 --> 00:08:07.920
And then you see it
on a Netflix special,

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:09.955
you're like, "I think
this guy's a lost cause."

00:08:09.955 --> 00:08:11.290
[ Laughter ]
-And I --

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:13.092
And I think I had
the ultimate call

00:08:13.092 --> 00:08:15.894
out of my doctor's offices,
which is I stepped on the scale

00:08:15.894 --> 00:08:17.396
when I went
to my doctor recently,

00:08:17.396 --> 00:08:19.732
and it's still the old-fashioned
abacus thing,

00:08:19.732 --> 00:08:22.167
like, you're not 1,000 pounds,
you're not 0 pounds,

00:08:22.167 --> 00:08:25.771
you're not 970 pounds,
you're not 21 pounds,

00:08:25.771 --> 00:08:29.241
you're not 650 pounds,
you're not 111 pounds.

00:08:29.241 --> 00:08:32.011
I'm like, "What time is it?"
You know, she's like,

00:08:32.011 --> 00:08:37.183
"It's not 2:00, it's not 6:00,
it's not 2:50, it's not 4:45."

00:08:37.183 --> 00:08:38.684
No, I don't know why they still
have those, but that's...

00:08:38.684 --> 00:08:40.953
-It's the best.
-Yeah.

00:08:40.953 --> 00:08:42.655
-I wanted to ask real quick
because I feel like

00:08:42.655 --> 00:08:46.525
we are both blessed
with very honest wives.

00:08:46.525 --> 00:08:49.395
I think your wife, Jen,
you've been married 15 years.

00:08:49.395 --> 00:08:51.196
I know she's very good
with feedback.

00:08:51.196 --> 00:08:52.831
Is that accurate?
-She's amazing.

00:08:52.831 --> 00:08:55.167
-And, you know, I think
that sometimes

00:08:55.167 --> 00:08:56.936
it's mostly upside
to have an honest wife.

00:08:56.936 --> 00:08:58.070
But like, sometimes,
you know...

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:00.506
-I think it's all dynamics,
right?

00:09:00.506 --> 00:09:03.175
Like, so in every --
We've been married 15 years.

00:09:03.175 --> 00:09:05.311
I think one of the upsides
of marriage is communication.

00:09:05.311 --> 00:09:07.513
So like you just have to --
there's so little

00:09:07.513 --> 00:09:11.817
you have to say words-wise
to convey an idea.

00:09:11.817 --> 00:09:14.586
Like, our friend Mulaney
a few years ago was like,

00:09:14.586 --> 00:09:16.922
"Hey, I'm going skydiving
for my birthday.

00:09:16.922 --> 00:09:18.023
Do you want to come?"

00:09:18.023 --> 00:09:19.157
And I was like,
"That sounds amazing.

00:09:19.157 --> 00:09:21.026
I'd never thought of doing
that in my life."

00:09:21.026 --> 00:09:24.162
And I went back to Jen and I
relayed this conversation to her

00:09:24.162 --> 00:09:26.031
and she said,
"You're going to do that?"

00:09:26.031 --> 00:09:28.033
And that's when I realized
I wasn't going to do that.

00:09:28.033 --> 00:09:30.169
[ Laughter ]
I said, "No way.

00:09:30.169 --> 00:09:31.937
Why would I want
to do an activity

00:09:31.937 --> 00:09:34.607
that seems so dangerous,
yet so fun?"

00:09:34.607 --> 00:09:36.342
Because ultimately,
I'm more afraid

00:09:36.342 --> 00:09:38.477
of my wife's judgment of me

00:09:38.477 --> 00:09:41.513
than I am of jumping out
of an airplane at 30,000 feet

00:09:41.513 --> 00:09:43.315
because both activities
are dangerous.

00:09:43.315 --> 00:09:45.584
But at the end of one of them,
you get to die.

00:09:45.584 --> 00:09:48.220
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:50.755
I'm very excited about this.
You grew up in Massachusetts.

00:09:50.755 --> 00:09:52.091
You're doing 10 shows.

00:09:52.091 --> 00:09:53.459
-At the Wilbur.
-At the Wilbur.

00:09:53.459 --> 00:09:56.796
One of the great stand-up
theaters in America.

00:09:56.796 --> 00:09:57.996
10 shows in a row in Boston.

00:09:57.996 --> 00:09:59.198
That must just be so exciting.

00:09:59.198 --> 00:10:01.867
-10 shows in Boston,
my hometown.

00:10:01.867 --> 00:10:03.235
Going to do --

00:10:03.235 --> 00:10:05.704
We're calling it
Christmas Parmesan.

00:10:05.704 --> 00:10:07.639
Because in my family,

00:10:07.639 --> 00:10:09.675
we didn't do a very religious
Christmas.

00:10:09.675 --> 00:10:12.778
If anything, the theme
was just chicken Parmesan.

00:10:12.778 --> 00:10:14.246
-Well, that's very exciting
for the people of Boston

